Let me think step by step. The naive O(n^2) approach scans every pair but it is too slow. A better approach is to walk the list once and keep a hash map from value to index. For each number at index i, I check if (target - nums[i]) is already in the map. If yes, I have found the answer. Otherwise I store nums[i] -> i and continue. This is O(n) time and O(n) space.

For the input Given nums = [2, 7, 11, 15] and target = 9, return the indices of the two numbers that add to 9. I will iterate the list once with the map.

```python
def two_sum(nums, target):
    seen = {}
    for i, x in enumerate(nums):
        if target - x in seen:
            return [seen[target - x], i]
        seen[x] = i
    return []
```

